Collections: labelled groupings of IRW tables (issue #1633).

A collection answers "give me all the tables of this type" -- RCTs, Big Five
inventories, depression scales, tables carrying a Q matrix. Tables belong to any
number of collections at once, so membership is stored long (one row per
table-collection pair), not as a column per collection.

WHY LONG FORMAT MATTERS: adding a collection is a DATA change. One line in
registry.csv, optionally one file in curated/. Nothing in ../metadata/10_collections.R
changes, and nothing in the R package, the Python package or the site changes.
A column-per-collection design would make every new collection an ~8-file change
across three repos. Do not add per-collection branches anywhere.

---------------------------------------------------------------- files

registry.csv          The spec. One row per collection, version controlled.
                      Columns: collection, label, kind, definition, rule,
                      maintainer, added.
curated/<slug>.csv    Hand-picked members for <slug>. Columns: table, basis,
                      note. Optional for rule-based collections (members are
                      unioned in); REQUIRED when rule = curated.
curated/*.review.csv  Staging only. Candidates a human has not promoted yet.
                      Never read by the pipeline -- purely a worklist.

Outputs land in ../metadata/ (collections.csv, collection_members.csv,
collections_report.txt) and are gitignored like every other pipeline output.
This directory holds the irreplaceable hand-authored input, which is why it sits
here and not under metadata/ -- src/.gitignore has `metadata/**/*.csv`.

---------------------------------------------------------------- kind

Exactly three values:

  design      how the study was run, and what structure the data carries.
              rct, clustered, q_matrix, continuous_response.
  instrument  which measure was administered. big_five, promis.
  construct   what is being measured. depression, math.

---------------------------------------------------------------- rule grammar

  var:treat                 the named variable is present
  var:date+wave             ALL named variables present            (+ = AND)
  var_any:booklet|block_id  ANY named variable present             (| = OR)
  var_prefix:qmatrix        any variable starting with the prefix
  meta:longitudinal         a logical column of metadata.csv
  cname:<regex>             case-insensitive regex over tags.csv "construct name"
  curated                   members come only from curated/<slug>.csv

Prefer `meta:` over re-deriving something metadata.csv already publishes. The
`longitudinal` collection reuses the published flag even though that flag is
loose (it greps the concatenated variable string, so cov_birthdate matches).
Two different answers to "is this table longitudinal?" would be worse than nine
loose rows.

---------------------------------------------------------------- derived fields

coverage, n_tables and basis are COMPUTED by 10_collections.R and must not be
authored in registry.csv -- authoring them is how they drift out of step with
the membership they describe.

  metadata-complete   rule read metadata.csv, so it saw every documented table.
                      NOT the same as "the whole warehouse": metadata.csv has no
                      rows for warehouses 5 and 6, and 403 tables are missing
                      from it. The script warns when configured core datasets
                      are absent.
  tagged-subset-only  rule read the `construct name` column of tags.csv, which
                      is filled for about half the tables (2,467 of 4,495 on
                      2026-09-23) and collapses in the newer warehouses (w1 98%,
                      w2 77%, w3 26%, w4 27%, w5 30%, w6 52%). Having a tags
                      row is not enough: most rows added by auto-tagging have no
                      construct name. These collections are biased toward older
                      warehouses and cannot honestly be called "all". The
                      searched count is appended to `definition` automatically.
  curated-only        a human chose the members.

---------------------------------------------------------------- adding one

1. Add a line to registry.csv. Write the definition as the inclusion criterion
   ("what counts"), not a description of the topic.
2. If it needs hand-picked members, add curated/<slug>.csv.
3. cd ../metadata && Rscript 10_collections.R
4. Read collections_report.txt. Check the count is what you expected and, for a
   cname: rule, spot-check the matched construct names -- a broad regex is the
   easy mistake. `\bmath` catches "mathematics anxiety".
5. Upload with the site-update skill's upload_meta.py.

---------------------------------------------------------------- exact-instrument collections

phq9, gad7, rses, cesd, dass21, panas, tipi, pcl5, gse, erq, mspss and sd3, then swls,
pss10, bfi44, bfi10, ucla_ls, fcv19s, lotr, isi and brief_cope (#1712, added 2026-09-30) answer "which tables administered THIS instrument?". They are `curated`, and they sit
beside the looser cname: collections (depression, self_esteem, affect_panas ...) rather than
replacing them.

What counts (Ben, 2026-09-30):
  - the full scale, or at least 80% of its items;
  - one whole subscale (a DASS depression-only table, a PANAS positive-affect-only table);
  - NOT a short form (PHQ-2, PHQ-8, GAD-2, CES-D-10, ...), a lengthened adaptation (the
    Chinese 14-item ERQ), or a reworded version (BrummerHoffman's "Augmented" PHQ-9/GAD-7).

`basis` starts with how identity was decided, and may carry a note after "; ":
  wording    the study's own item text (provenance text_source = study_materials) matches the
             instrument's wording. Identity is observed.
  supplied   IRW's item-text pipeline identified the instrument and supplied its canonical
             wording (canonical_instrument, translated_substitute, or no provenance row), so a
             wording match only confirms IRW's own call. Identity is asserted.
  name       no usable English item text; only the metadata names the instrument.
  e.g. "supplied; subscale: anxiety (7/21)", "name; partial: 18/20 items".
The note travels inside `basis` because read_curated() publishes only table and basis. For the
strictly verified set, filter on basis starting with "wording". It is small: 29 of the
188 first-batch memberships, and 2 of the 82 in the second batch.

How membership was built, and how to redo it:
  1. python3 collections/scout_instruments.py [slug ...]
       Candidates are tables whose construct name / item-text instrument field names the
       instrument, or a whole table-name token does. Each is judged on its item text against
       the instrument's key: the k most common normalised wordings among the candidates
       (printed to curated/<slug>.key.txt; CHECK IT. With under ~3 texts the key is garbage:
       PSS's came out as a nursing-stressor scale). Writes curated/<slug>.review.csv.
  2. python3 collections/presort_instruments.py
       Applies the rules above -> curated/instruments_presort.csv (ACCEPT / REJECT / DECIDE,
       with a reason on every row). Rows the rules leave open get a hand call, with its
       reason, in curated/instruments_overrides.csv.
  3. A human reviews the presort; the ACCEPT rows become curated/<slug>.csv (table, basis,
     note). A table absent from metadata.csv can't be a member (10_collections.R stops).
     That cuts both ways: WITHDRAWING a table that a curated file names makes the next pipeline
     run stop at this stage. When a table is withdrawn, remove it from every curated/*.csv
     (grep -l <table> collections/curated/*.csv) in the same PR. saha_2026_cesd was the first case.
The second batch (swls, pss10, bfi44, bfi10, ucla_ls, fcv19s, lotr, isi, brief_cope; added
2026-09-30) had too little item text for a derived key: their <slug>.key.txt files are NOT the
instruments (pss10's is a nursing-stressor scale, bfi10's is German), so ignore them. Those nine
were decided on metadata instead: item count, table name and instrument / construct-name fields,
with a hand call and its reason in instruments_overrides.csv wherever the rules didn't settle a
row. Most members are therefore `name`. SWLS has no item text in IRW at all (withheld on rights
grounds), so all 22 of its members are `name`. Don't "fix" this by writing full canonical wordings
into a key file: that would publish, in this repo, item text IRW deliberately doesn't release.
Two calls in this batch stretch the rules and are recorded as such:
  - lotr: a table with the LOT-R's 6 scored items counts ("scored items: 6/10"), because the
    other 4 items are unscored fillers, not part of the scale.
  - ucla_ls: the 1980 revision and Version 3 (1996) are both 20 items but word them differently.
    Both count; the definition says so.

---------------------------------------------------------------- two invariants

1. MEMBERSHIP IS CONFIRMED-ONLY. There is no candidate/status column. Scouting
   output stays in curated/*.review.csv until a human promotes it. A published
   collection is one somebody stands behind.

2. THIS STAGE NEVER OPENS THE TAGS GOOGLE SHEET. cname: rules read the already
   published tags.csv, whose "construct name" is column 3 of 03_tags.R's
   positional KEEP_COLS. Column 4 ("Context Text" -- verbatim excerpts from
   source papers) is structurally unreachable from here, and that is a
   guarantee, not a convention. Do not "improve" the stage by reading the sheet
   directly -- any script that needs column 4 has to justify itself separately,
   and this one does not need it at all.
